  "notes": "St. Louis minimalism — \"this bench is defined by subtraction\", and the model is written as subtraction. One bell cell of two to four pitches (`motifPitchCount` 3, `densityPerBar` [1,3], `emptyBarBias` 0.3) over 1- and 2-bar loops, no pad and no counter-line, against the sparsest kick here at 2–3 hits a bar and a thin 16th hat stream with restrained rolls. What is left is the 808: a single very prominent `heavy` one-shot with short glides, the arrangement's main voice, sitting at the bottom of the register. `phraseBars` reaches 1 as well as 2 because the research names 1-bar loops specifically. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates this high-medium. ⚠ INFERRED: the `i–VII` and `i–VI` families and the bpm `mode` of 140. The research describes a static tonic or no pitched instrument at all; those two low-weight families exist so the bass has more than one root to reach and the chord part can still make its distinct-output floor, and they are the first thing to delete if this ever sounds like it has changes. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts. ⚠ **The 1-bar phrase length was removed** (was [1,2], now [2,4]). A one-bar phrase looped four times carries less entropy than a four-bar phrase, and it is the countermelody it strangles hardest: a counter answers *inside* the melody’s phrase, so a one-bar shape leaves it nowhere to go. Every worst offender on `counter.rs` carried it. ⛔ The previous session recorded this exact mistake and it arrived again from the other direction — distinctness is combinatorial in the shape LENGTH, not in the option count.",
